# For more information on these fields, see:
# https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/docs/adding_to_third_party.md#readme_chromium
#
# TaffyGo vendors the flag-icons *recipe* under //taffy rather than under
# Chromium's own //third_party because the corpus is a TaffyGo product
# decision, not an upstream one: decision 0047 records why the shipped set is
# exactly ISO 3166-1 and why the licence travels inside the pack. No artwork is
# committed here. The pin in tools/manifest.json names the npm tarball and its
# SHA-256, and tools/build_flag_pack.py rasterises and packages it.

Name: flag-icons
Short Name: flag-icons
URL: https://registry.npmjs.org/flag-icons/-/flag-icons-7.5.0.tgz
Version: 7.5.0
Update Mechanism: Manual
License: MIT
License File: vendor/flag-icons.MIT.txt
Shipped: yes
Security Critical: no

Description:
The country-flag artwork screens SCR-001, SCR-006 and SCR-407 draw. Built from
the pinned tarball into one zip of 249 WebP members plus LICENSE, catalogued
for the delivery plane as asset `country-flags` revision `7.5.0-taffy.1`. The
recipe lives here; the built zip is committed at
taffy-core/components/delivery/bundled/flags-4x3-webp.zip.

Shipped is "yes" because the APK carries the pack (decision 0202):
//taffy/components/delivery:bundled_delivery_assets packages that zip, and it
is seeded into the delivery store on first run. Nothing fetches it from a
host. Decision 0206 needs this field to be true: the credits page is generated
from it, and a part recorded as not shipped is left out of the notice the
package carries.

Local Modifications:
None to the upstream artwork. The build keeps only the ISO 3166-1 alpha-2
intersection of the 4x3 set, rasterises through a pinned librsvg/cairo pair,
and encodes with a pinned cwebp. tools/iso-country-codes.tsv is first-party
and is the list decision 0047 named.
